package landings

import (
	"github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2"
	"journal/internal/web"
)

templ AboutPage(c *fiber.Ctx) {
	@web.Base(c) {
		<section class="landing" id="about-page">
			<h2>About This Journal</h2>
			<p>
				Contour Journal started as something I built for myself.
			</p>
			<p>
				I wanted a place to write — not to be analyzed, prompted, or nudged toward productivity. Just to process what was happening, notice what I was grateful for, and figure out what I actually wanted to do next. I looked at the apps that existed and kept running into the same problems: too minimal to be useful, too complex to feel calm, or asking me to hand over my writing to a platform that would do something with it I didn't fully understand or agree with.
			</p>
			<p>
				So I built this instead.
			</p>
			<h3>What it’s for</h3>
			<p>
				At its core, this is a space for freeform writing with a little structure around it — a rating, a topic, a place to jot down small actions and things worth being thankful for. The idea is that reflection is useful, but what comes out of it matters more than the entries themselves.
			</p>
			<p>
				Over time, those small pieces add up to something you can actually see: which days tend to feel better, how gratitude tracks with mood, whether the actions you write down are ones you actually take.
			</p>
			<h3>What it's not</h3>
			<p>
				This isn't a therapy tool. It won't analyze your writing, suggest how you should feel, or remind you that you missed a day. There are no streaks, no gamification, and no AI reading your entries.
			</p>
			<p>
				The absence of those things is intentional.
			</p>
			<h3>Privacy, for real</h3>
			<p>
				All journal entries, actions, and thankfuls are encrypted. There are no ads, no marketing cookies, no data sharing. Your writing isn't used to train anything or sold to anyone. There's a blur mode if you're writing somewhere public.
			</p>
			<p>
				None of that is a feature — it's just how a journal should work.
			</p>
			<h3>An ongoing project</h3>
			<p>
				This is actively maintained and slowly growing. New visualizations and features get added when they feel genuinely useful, not just because they could be built.
			</p>
			<p>
				If it helps you slow down, see a pattern you wouldn't have noticed, or take one small step you might have otherwise skipped — it's doing its job.
			</p>
		</section>
	}
}
